  "id": "push-press",
  "name": "Push Press",
  "definition": "A standing whole-body power press performed from a front rack. A preparatory push-press countermovement precedes rapid hip extension, knee extension, and ankle plantarflexion that propel the bar upward while the shoulder flexes and abducts in a front-scapular path, the scapula rotates upward and tilts posteriorly, and the elbow extends. After the lower-body taxonomy split, vasti remain the primary knee-extension region while rectus femoris receives conservative secondary credit; gastrocnemius and soleus are independently required secondaries for the declared plantarflexion action. Those individual lower-body roles combine joint-level push-press kinetics with the anatomy capability map rather than claiming exercise-specific muscle ranking. The athlete keeps the feet in contact with the floor and finishes standing without a second receiving dip. Strict overhead presses, behind-neck presses, thrusters, push jerks, split jerks, landmine presses, and unreviewed dumbbell, kettlebell, log, or Viking-press variants remain outside this family.",
